Thus we can use establish_clearance_nominal for beta after we have loading verified, compliance certificate linked, loading container linked, leg linked, and not origin_leg_loaded nor destination_leg_loaded (we haven't loaded either leg via container). Good.

Now we need candidate_finalized shift_candidate_beta. There are two finalize actions: finalize_candidate_after_compliance_validation (requires compliance_validation_complete ?candidate) and finalize_candidate_after_sequence_validation (requires candidate_clearance_established and route_sequence_validated). Also finalize_candidate_direct (requires candidate_clearance_established and not route_sequence_context_set). Since we will have clearance established, we could use finalize_candidate_after_sequence_validation if we also have route_sequence_validated. Or we could use finalize_candidate_direct if we avoid route_sequence_context_set. However finalize_candidate_direct requires not route_sequence_context_set ?candidate. We will have route_sequence_context_set ?candidate as effect of establish_clearance_nominal? Actually establish_clearance_nominal effect only sets candidate_clearance_established ?candidate. It does not set route_sequence_context_set. So after establish_clearance_nominal, route_sequence_context_set remains false (if initially false). Let's check init: route_context not mentioned for beta. So likely false. So we could use finalize_candidate_direct which requires candidate_clearance_established and not route_sequence_context_set. That would set candidate_finalized and dispatch_ready. That seems simpler: we don't need to validate route sequence.

Thus after establishing clearance, we can finalize_candidate_direct shift_candidate_beta.

That will set candidate_finalized and dispatch_ready.

